A||||4||Cliff Pennock||Problem with my brakes
Z||000000||Cliff Pennock||09-05-99||02:39 PM||||Yesterday, something happened that scared the crap out of me... First of all, it was a very hot day here, around 80F and my engine got a little hot too (I have a small coolant leak which will be fixed soon). I do think this is related to the brake problem although I don't yet see how.<p>Anyway, I was driving and had to slow down for an upcoming traffic-light and when I hit the brakes, the pedal almost went completely to the floor before it engaged! It did brake normally although I had to press the pedal <b>much</b> further down than normal. My reaction (thanks to years of karting) was to immediately release the brakes and try again (pumping). The second time it braked normally. When the car stopped, I pressed the brake as hard as I could to see if it was loosing pressure but it didn't. It happened a few times more but only after I hadn't used the brakes for more than a minute or so.<p>Could this be related to the engine's heat? That the brake-fluid near the engine got so hot that it looses its coherence? Should I be worried or will this problem disappear as soon as I have fixed the coolant leak?
Z||000001||Cooter||09-05-99||03:54 PM||||I had a similiar problem with my car a while back. No fluid leaks, but the pedal would sink after a few minutes of non-use. Turned out to be the master cylinder needed a rebuild. One seal was allowing fluid to get by under light braking, but under hard braking it was fine. We bled brakes for days before finally disassembling the master cylinder and finding the worn seal.
Z||000002||batboy||09-06-99||09:04 PM||||I'm with Cooter on this one. Sounds like master cylinder trouble. This has happened on more than one of my old cars in the past and it has always been the master cylinder if there were no leaks.
Z||000003||lowCG||09-06-99||10:16 PM||||Ahh,the karting days.............<br>Oh,anyways,I do believe you're in the need of a rebuilt master cylinder on this one,indeed,before the next episode.<br>Small pieces of oxidised crap tend to foul the ability of the seals and,well,you know.
Z||000004||lowCG||09-06-99||10:21 PM||||Oh,yeah;flush the system as well,and you'll have less to fuss over in the future,maybe even change over to synthetic fluid.I never had a problem in my old Mini Cooper with that stuff,and everyone told me how much trouble I was in for(with that car).
